 "limitations": {
  "stage3": [
   "Only the plain `attention` variant is patched and exercised, per reference_output_configuration. GatedAttention is not covered.",
   "The original has no validation set or model selection. The fixture's `val` bags are written to `bag_lists/val_unused.csv` but never seen by the original code. exact.selection holds per-epoch train loss and train error, with the last epoch marked as selected.",
   "The epochs are shortened (3 and 2) instead of the repository default of 20, to keep the per-step arrays well under 50 MB. lr 5e-4 and reg 1e-4 are the repository defaults.",
   "The LeNet image encoder and the MNIST/torchvision bag construction in `dataloader._create_bags` (including `np.int`) never run. The reference only covers the feature-input path the lab chose and makes no claim about MNIST accuracy.",
   "Fixture bags have no instance labels. The adapter passes -1, which the original only prints during `test()`.",
   "The positive class is `class_order[1]` (fixture label 1 becomes bag label True). The class_order mapping in the WSIP adapter is not exercised here.",
   "The fixtures are too small to drive p outside [1e-5, 1-1e-5], so the clamp's zero-gradient regime is not exercised.",
   "CPU only, one thread, deterministic algorithms, on the modern pinned torch/numpy in the WSIP reference venv, not the README's PyTorch 0.3.1 / Python 2.7."
  ],
  "stage5": [
   "Parity is shown only on tiny synthetic CPU feature bags (8-12 dimensions, 6-10 bags, 2-3 epochs) with one thread and deterministic algorithms. It says nothing about GPU or multi-threaded numerics, real pathology feature sizes, or the full 20-epoch default schedule.",
   "The reference is the patched original (feature-input Linear+ReLU in place of LeNet, fixture bags in place of MNIST-bags), so parity does not validate those lab decisions themselves, all still PROVISIONAL. The gated_attention variant has no evidence and is not covered.",
   "Two aggregates are rebuilt in the test from the candidate's own per-step values using main.py's formulas: epoch train loss and test mean loss, which main.py accumulates in float32. WSIP's session history keeps a float64 mean, which is not compared against the evidence.",
   "Test splits have only 2 bags per case, so the final decision and threshold behaviour is exercised on very few predictions; the threshold mutation is detected through the exported threshold value, not necessarily through a changed prediction.",
   "WSIP's per-epoch validation pass (with isolated RNG) has no counterpart in the original and is checked only indirectly: the sample order and every later step still match exactly."
  ]
 }
